 "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-11-15T20:23:15Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#43756390490", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Agustín Leonardo was a Spanish painter of the Baroque period.", "newValue": "Agustín Leonardo (1580, Tarazona – 1640) was a prominent Spanish Baroque painter whose devotional works significantly contributed to the visual culture of the Counter-Reformation in early 17th-century Spain. Active primarily in Valencia and Madrid, Leonardo established himself through large-scale religious commissions, embodying the dramatic intensity and chiaroscuro prevalent in the Spanish Golden Age. His artistic output, characterized by its fervent piety and robust figural compositions, placed him among the respected masters who shaped the aesthetic landscape of a nation deeply invested in its Catholic identity.

Leonardo's most notable achievement is the extensive 'Life of Saint Jerome' series, executed for the main cloister of the Hieronymite Monastery in Madrid between 1626 and 1627. This monumental cycle, comprising eleven large canvases, meticulously narrates episodes from the life of the influential Church Father, serving as both spiritual instruction and artistic spectacle for the monastic community and wider public. Beyond this major work, Leonardo produced numerous altarpieces and devotional panels, often exhibiting the strong influence of contemporary Madrid court painters such such as Vicente Carducho and Eugenio Caxés, while maintaining a distinctive personal style marked by expressive brushwork and profound emotional depth.

